 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "comeraghs_boyle",
   "title": "Robert Boyle",
   "text": "Robert Boyle was born at Lismore Castle, south-west of here on the Blackwater, in 1627, the fourteenth child of the Earl of Cork. With an air pump built by Robert Hooke, he showed that a gas squeezed into half the space pushes back twice as hard: Boyle's Law, published in 1662. He helped found the Royal Society and wrote The Sceptical Chymist, which helped turn alchemy into chemistry."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "comeraghs_walton",
   "title": "Ernest Walton",
   "text": "Ernest Walton was born in 1903 in Dungarvan, on the coast below these mountains, the son of a Methodist minister. In 1932, at the Cavendish Laboratory in Cambridge, he and John Cockcroft fired a beam of protons at lithium and split its atoms: the first atoms ever split by a machine. They shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1951. Walton taught physics at Trinity College Dublin for decades."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 41,
   "text": "COUMSHINGAUN. A glacial corrie, a deep armchair of rock scooped out by ice, with a lake at the bottom. In Irish, Com Seangan, 'hollow of the ants'. The ants are long gone, but the view is not."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "THE CORRIE WALK. Steep, wet, and spectacular. Don't go up in fog. The cliffs don't announce themselves."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "MAHON FALLS. An 80 m cascade down the mountainside, which after rain looks like the mountain has had a good cry. On a dry day it's a sulk."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "A STANDING STONE. The Comeraghs are full of old stones, tombs and field walls. Some of the walls were built last week. It's hard to tell."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "comeraghs_magic_road",
   "title": "The Magic Road",
   "text": "On the road up to Mahon Falls there is a stretch where a car left in neutral seems to roll uphill. It's an optical illusion: the lie of the land makes a gentle downhill look like a climb. Locals will happily let you believe it's the púca."
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 13,
   "plaque": "comeraghs_crotty",
   "title": "Crotty's Rock",
   "text": "In the 1700s the highwayman William Crotty robbed travellers on the roads round the Comeraghs and hid in a cave on the cliffs above the lake that now bears his name, Crotty's Lake. He was caught and hanged in Waterford. The rock still keeps a lookout."
  }
 ],
